# Expectation record for 03_initializers/001_scalar_and_array.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 001_scalar_and_array
area               = 03_initializers
description        = Baseline scalar, array and struct initialization, each form declared once with static and once with automatic storage duration, every element and member read back on its own line
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour, which is the precondition that
makes both differential oracles sound: undefined behaviour would license either compiler to do
anything at all, so a divergence over it would prove nothing about either one.
A member or element left unmentioned by a brace-enclosed initializer is initialized as an object
of static storage duration would be, that is to zero, for automatic and static duration alike
(C11 6.7.9p19 and p21), so every value printed has a determinate value and no uninitialized
storage is ever read. The file-scope objects additionally have static storage duration and are
zero-initialized before their initializers apply. Every object here is FULLY initialized by its
initializer list before any part of it is read, so the zero guarantee is not even relied upon:
every one of g_scalar, g_arr, g_u_arr, g_pt, l_scalar, l_arr and l_pt is completely covered by
its own initializer. Nothing is modified after initialization, so no object is modified twice
between two sequence points and no modification races a read of the same object.
No arithmetic is performed on any initialized value at all. The only arithmetic in the program is
the three loop counters' increments, bounded by the literals 5, 3 and 5, so signed overflow is
unreachable. Nothing is shifted, so no shift-count question arises. Nothing is divided, so no
division by zero and no remainder question arises.
Every subscript is in bounds and provably so, because every bound is a literal that matches the
array's declared extent: g_arr and l_arr are int[5] read at 0 through 4, and g_u_arr is
unsigned int[3] read at 0 through 2. No pointer is formed anywhere in the program -- not even a
one-past-the-end pointer, which would be legal to form but is not needed here -- so no pointer
arithmetic is performed, no address is taken and none is printed.
There is no aliasing violation, because every object is read through an lvalue of its own
declared type: the int arrays and members through int, the unsigned int array through
unsigned int. No object representation is inspected, no union is present, and no cast of any kind
appears in the program, so no effective-type rule is approached, no padding byte is observed and
nothing depends on the relative addresses of unrelated objects.
The widest initializer is 4294967295u, which is exactly UINT_MAX wherever unsigned int is 32 bits
-- all four supported targets -- so it is representable in the type it initializes and no
implicit conversion changes its value. -9 initializes an int member and is representable in int
on every target the suite supports.
Each printf call receives arguments that are a loop counter and one array element or struct
member; none of them has a side effect, so the unspecified order in which a function call's
arguments are evaluated cannot be observed. Every conversion specifier matches its argument's
type after the default argument promotions: %d takes int, %u takes unsigned int, and both are
four bytes on all four targets, so no variadic argument is read at the wrong width.
main returns 0, inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is truncated by
the operating system. Nothing outside this file is read: every input is a literal here, no file,
socket, environment variable, clock, random source or locale is consulted, and iteration order is
fixed by literal loop bounds, so every cell is reproducible from this source and this record
alone.
No header is included anywhere and printf is declared by hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`.
bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a bonus stdatomic.h and ships no standard
I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so naming one would fail on the bcc side of
oracle (a) while succeeding on the reference side -- a spurious divergence caused by the test
rather than by a compiler. This area is granted no header exception; the corpus has exactly two,
area 07's stdarg.h and the dedicated bundled-header probe in area 12.
This record declares no warning-gate deviation, and area 03 sanctions none. The program is clean
under the full default gate -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow
-Werror at -O0, -O1 and -O2 with all four reference drivers, and it builds and runs clean under
the sanitizer gate -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all. Both gates are
reference-compiler-only and are never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline
is untouched.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows nothing: all four targets, all three optimization levels and all three
oracles are enabled, no expected-divergence marker is carried, and no implementation-defined
value reaches this program's output.
WIDTH NORMALIZATION. Every printed value has type int or unsigned int, both four bytes on all
four targets, and %d and %u are the only conversion specifiers in the file. No type whose width
varies by target -- long, size_t, ptrdiff_t, intptr_t, any pointer, any floating type -- is
declared, computed or printed, and no length modifier for a wide type appears. That is what keeps
the i686 cell comparable with the other three: i686 is ELF32 with a four-byte long and a
four-byte pointer while x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64 are ELF64 with eight bytes for both
(docs/technical-specifications.md target table, lines 457-462), a difference also MEASURED on all
four targets in this environment. 4294967295u is exactly UINT_MAX wherever unsigned int is 32
bits, which is all four targets, so the widest initializer in the file is representable with no
conversion.
PLAIN-CHAR SIGNEDNESS. No character data appears at all, so the MEASURED split -- plain char
signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64 -- cannot reach the output.
PADDING AND LAYOUT. Members are read individually by name; no object is printed, copied or
reinterpreted as bytes, so padding, whose contents the standard leaves unspecified, is never
observed, and these all-int aggregates carry no internal padding on any of the four targets in any
case. No sizeof, no _Alignof and no address is printed, so no layout decision the ABI is free to
make is observable here. Only the values the initializers supply are.
EXECUTION CHARACTER SET. No character or string literal value is printed, so C11 5.2.1 leaves
nothing here to the implementation.
THE DISCRIMINATING VARIANT. Every form is initialized twice, once at file scope and once inside a
block, so the pair isolates storage duration rather than also varying the type: a single struct
definition serves both. A static-duration initializer is a translation-time constant placed in the
program's data image, while the same initializer at automatic duration must be materialized by
code emitted on entry to the block, and both paths are compiled at all three optimization levels.
Nineteen lines are printed and nothing is summarized, because one wrong slot must produce exactly
one divergent line -- that is what localizes a defect to a single initializer and keeps
minimization tractable.
MEASURED EVIDENCE. The program was compiled with the reference driver for all four targets at
-O0, -O1 and -O2 -- twelve configurations -- and every one produced these 19 lines byte-identically
with exit status 0, which is why oracle (b) compares this program's targets against each other
without any narrowing and why oracle (c) can hold these bytes as the golden record.
The one property that would legitimately change these bytes is an unsigned int narrower or wider
than 32 bits, which would change what 4294967295u denotes. That would be an implementation-defined
divergence rather than a compiler defect, and must then be recorded as an expected divergence with
this record's target list narrowed and the reason stated in this field -- never left to surface as
a finding against a backend that did nothing wrong.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
g_scalar=42
g_arr[0]=10
g_arr[1]=20
g_arr[2]=30
g_arr[3]=40
g_arr[4]=50
g_u_arr[0]=0
g_u_arr[1]=1
g_u_arr[2]=4294967295
g_pt.x=7
g_pt.y=-9
l_scalar=42
l_arr[0]=10
l_arr[1]=20
l_arr[2]=30
l_arr[3]=40
l_arr[4]=50
l_pt.x=7
l_pt.y=-9
END
